---
import LatexLogo from '../components/LatexLogo.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeriesTool from '../components/SeriesTool.svelte';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={'Taylor & Maclaurin series | lab by faris0x'}
  description="Expand a function as a Taylor or Maclaurin series, see every derivative and coefficient, and export the working as a .tex file."
  current="series"
  math
>
  <div class="tool-head">
    <div>
      <h1>Taylor &amp; Maclaurin series</h1>
    </div>
    <p>
      Expand a function about a point, inspect each derivative and coefficient,
      and export the finished series as <LatexLogo />. Setting the centre to
      zero gives the Maclaurin series.
    </p>
  </div>

  <SeriesTool client:idle />

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="method">
    <h2 id="method">How the expansion is built</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      The Taylor series of a function about <span class="inline-code">x = a</span>
      is the polynomial whose coefficients are
      <span class="inline-code">c<sub>k</sub> = f<sup>(k)</sup>(a) / k!</span>.
      The tool differentiates symbolically, evaluates each derivative at the
      centre, and divides by the factorial.
    </p>
    <p class="section-intro">
      When <span class="inline-code">a = 0</span> the same construction is
      called the Maclaurin series. The degree you choose is the highest power
      kept; the table shows every term from degree zero upwards.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="syntax">
    <h2 id="syntax">Syntax</h2>
    <ul class="plain-list section-intro">
      <li><strong>Variable</strong>: <code>x</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Operators</strong>: <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code> and <code>^</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Functions</strong>: <code>sin</code>, <code>cos</code>, <code>tan</code>, <code>exp</code>, <code>ln</code>, <code>sqrt</code>, <code>sinh</code> and <code>cosh</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Constants</strong>: <code>pi</code> and <code>e</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Implicit multiplication</strong>: <code>2x</code>, <code>x(x+1)</code> and <code>2sin(x)</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="exactness">
    <h2 id="exactness">Exactness</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Coefficients are exact rational numbers whenever the derivatives evaluate
      to rational numbers. At a non-zero centre, transcendental values stay
      symbolic: the Taylor series of <span class="inline-code">sin x</span>
      about <span class="inline-code">x = 1</span> contains
      <span class="inline-code">sin(1)</span> and
      <span class="inline-code">cos(1)</span> rather than decimal
      approximations.
    </p>
    <p class="section-intro">
      The centre must be a rational number, and the degree is limited to
      twelve so the derivative expressions stay readable. The tool does not
      estimate a remainder or a radius of convergence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="export">
    <h2 id="export">Export</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      The <LatexLogo /> tab previews the document, and the export bar copies or
      downloads the same source. It uses <code>amsmath</code> and
      <code>amssymb</code>, so a standard TeX installation compiles the
      derivative table and the final series.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
